{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Implementation Toolkit","title":"AI Policy Language for Students","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting ai policy language for students to AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Contrast deterrence and transparency models of academic integrity and explain why generative AI makes deterrence unenforceable.","Write an AI-use clause with a bounded permission, a one-line disclosure format with a worked example, and an accessibility carve-out.","Apply the distinguishability, disclosure-cost, and accessibility checks to evaluate a draft AI-use policy.","Predict the behavioral effect (concealment, exclusion, or transparency) a policy clause will produce."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit","ail606_supplement_ai_policy_language_for_students","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/08/03"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_calibration"]},"content":"# AI Policy Language for Students\n\n![AI Policy Language for Students textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/08-04-ai-policy-language-for-students.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn AI-use policy is not a warning label; it is an instrument that shapes behavior. The words you choose determine whether students conceal their tool use or disclose it, whether assistive-technology users feel criminalized, and whether you retain any visibility into how AI shaped a learner's design judgment. Generative AI made the old binary, allowed or forbidden, unworkable, because the same tool that drafts an essay also reads alt text aloud and critiques a learner's own draft. A policy that cannot distinguish those uses governs nothing it intends to.\n\nThis matters because the wrong policy produces the opposite of its goal. A flat prohibition does not eliminate AI use; it drives it underground, where the instructor loses the ability to assess where the student's judgment ends and the tool's begins. The design problem in this section is to write language that makes the boundary of student authorship visible rather than hidden, which is the only form of integrity an instructor can actually observe and teach.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Contrast deterrence and transparency models of academic integrity and explain why generative AI makes deterrence unenforceable.\n- Write an AI-use clause with a bounded permission, a one-line disclosure format with a worked example, and an accessibility carve-out.\n- Apply the distinguishability, disclosure-cost, and accessibility checks to evaluate a draft AI-use policy.\n- Predict the behavioral effect (concealment, exclusion, or transparency) a policy clause will produce.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAn instructor, alarmed by a viral story about AI cheating, adds one line to the syllabus: \"Use of AI tools such as ChatGPT is prohibited; violations are academic dishonesty.\" The term unfolds in two quiet failures. A student who relies on an AI screen-reader for alt text does not know whether her assistive tool counts, and stops using it during coursework rather than risk an accusation. Three other students use AI to brainstorm, decide it is safer not to mention it, and hand in work whose origins the instructor can no longer see. The ban did not produce integrity; it produced silence and exclusion.\n\nThe case reveals the central mechanism: a prohibition cannot distinguish a learner who generated a whole storyboard from one who used AI to critique their own narration, so it treats both as the same crime and pushes both into hiding. As you read, watch how replacing prohibition with bounded permission plus disclosure changes not the students' honesty but the instructor's visibility into it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nGood AI-use policy language rests on a shift from a deterrence model to a transparency model of integrity. Deterrence asks \"how do I stop the prohibited act,\" and it works only when the act is detectable; with generative AI it is not reliably detectable, so deterrence collapses into either false accusations or undetected use. Transparency asks \"how do I keep the learner's judgment visible,\" and it works precisely because the learner, not a detector, supplies the disclosure.\n\nA workable policy has three structural parts:\n\n- **Bounded permission.** Name the supported uses (brainstorming options, critiquing one's own draft, generating alternatives to react to) and the prohibited one (submitting AI-generated design work as your own). A boundary the student can apply beats a blanket the student must guess around.\n- **Disclosure with a worked example.** Require a one-line disclosure naming the tool, the prompt intent, and what was accepted or rejected, and show one filled-in example. Without a model, students invent formats under pressure, raising the very load that suppresses disclosure.\n- **Alignment with the learning goal.** The policy should protect the construct being assessed (design judgment), not all AI contact. Banning an assistive reader protects nothing and excludes someone.\n\nA subtle but decisive principle: policy language is also an accessibility decision. Assistive technologies increasingly embed AI, so any blanket ban silently penalizes disabled students. A policy that forgets this is not just harsh; it is likely noncompliant.\n\nMatch each policy stance to the behavior it actually produces and the visibility of student judgment it leaves you.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Flat prohibition\",\"definition\":\"Concealment plus accidental exclusion of assistive-technology users; use goes underground and judgment is invisible\"},{\"term\":\"Unlimited permission\",\"definition\":\"AI-authored work submitted as the student own; the construct (design judgment) is never assessed\"},{\"term\":\"Bounded permission plus disclosure\",\"definition\":\"Disclosed, bounded use the instructor can assess; the boundary of student judgment is on the page\"},{\"term\":\"Accessibility carve-out\",\"definition\":\"Assistive technologies a student relies on are always permitted and need no disclosure, so the policy protects rather than excludes\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n| Policy stance | What it actually produces | Visibility of student judgment |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Flat prohibition | Concealment plus accidental exclusion of AT users | None; use goes underground |\n| Unlimited permission | AI-authored work submitted as the student's | None; the construct is not assessed |\n| Bounded permission plus disclosure | Disclosed, bounded use the instructor can assess | High; the boundary is on the page |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one concrete revision of the AI-use clause an instructor places in a design course syllabus. The artifact is the policy paragraph students read before they touch any AI tool.\n\n**Before (the prohibition clause).** The original clause read: \"Use of AI tools such as ChatGPT is prohibited. Violations are academic dishonesty.\" When this policy ran for one term, two failures appeared: a student who used a screen-reader's AI alt-text generator did not know whether that counted, and three students used AI for brainstorming and hid it, so the instructor lost any view of how AI shaped their drafts. A flat ban produced concealment, not integrity.\n\n1. **Decide what the policy is actually protecting.** The goal is not zero AI; it is that the learner's design judgment remains theirs and is visible. A ban cannot distinguish a learner who generated a whole storyboard from one who used AI to critique their own narration.\n2. **Replace prohibition with bounded permission plus disclosure.** The clause becomes: \"You may use AI for bounded support (brainstorming options, critiquing your own draft, generating alternatives to react to). You may not submit AI-generated design work as your own. For any AI-assisted decision, add a one-line disclosure naming the tool, the prompt intent, and what you accepted or rejected.\"\n3. **Show the disclosure format so it lowers, not raises, student load.** A single worked disclosure line is included: \"Used Claude to generate three signaling alternatives for Frame 2; accepted one, rejected two because they added redundant on-screen text.\" By **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)**, a concrete template removes the load of inventing a disclosure format under pressure.\n\n**After (the policy as a transparency contract).** The revised clause permits bounded use, forbids submitting generated work as one's own, and requires a one-line disclosure with an example. In the next term, disclosures appeared on 11 of 12 AI-assisted drafts and the instructor could see and assess the boundary of student judgment. The change is auditable: a reviewer can compare \"prohibited / dishonesty\" to the bounded-permission clause and the disclosures it produced.\n\nWork the dilemma a real student faces under each policy choice. Trace where each clause sends the behavior.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Will this clause produce integrity or concealment?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A student used AI to generate three signaling options for Frame 2 and kept one. Your draft policy says only \\\"AI tools are prohibited; violations are academic dishonesty.\\\" What does the student most likely do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Disclose the bounded use openly\",\"next\":\"conceal\",\"feedback\":\"Unlikely. A flat ban offers no safe disclosure path, so honest students hide bounded use rather than risk an accusation.\"},{\"label\":\"Stay silent and submit the draft\",\"next\":\"conceal\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly the failure mode: the ban relocates the behavior to where you cannot see it.\"}]},\"conceal\":{\"prompt\":\"You now lose visibility into where the student judgment ends and the tool begins. You revise the clause. Which move best restores visibility?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Make the ban firmer and add penalties\",\"next\":\"firmer\",\"feedback\":\"Firmness is not enforceability. A ban on an undetectable act is loud but empty and deepens concealment.\"},{\"label\":\"Permit bounded use and require a one-line disclosure with an example\",\"next\":\"bounded\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The learner supplies the disclosure, so the boundary becomes visible and assessable.\"},{\"label\":\"Allow unlimited AI use with no disclosure\",\"next\":\"unlimited\",\"feedback\":\"This hides the construct a different way: AI-authored work arrives unmarked and design judgment is never assessed.\"}]},\"firmer\":{\"prompt\":\"Disclosure rates stay near zero and an assistive-technology user stops using her AI screen reader for fear of violating the rule. What did the firmer ban actually buy?\",\"feedback\":\"Nothing it intended: more concealment plus accidental exclusion. Strength of wording is not control over outcomes.\",\"choices\":[]},\"unlimited\":{\"prompt\":\"A storyboard arrives that is entirely AI-generated, indistinguishable on the page from bounded use. Can you assess design judgment?\",\"feedback\":\"No. Without a disclosed boundary you cannot tell critique-of-own-draft from wholesale generation, so the construct goes unassessed.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bounded\":{\"prompt\":\"Disclosures appear on 11 of 12 AI-assisted drafts and you add one sentence: assistive technologies a student relies on are always permitted and need no disclosure. What have you achieved?\",\"feedback\":\"A transparency contract: visible, bounded, accessible. Integrity is now something you can observe and teach rather than merely forbid.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite the policy to protect the learning construct, not to ban a category of tool. Every clause should be testable against one question: does this rule keep the student's design judgment visible, or does it merely forbid something a detector cannot reliably catch?\n\nRun any AI-use clause through three checks before you adopt it:\n\n1. **Distinguishability check:** Can the policy tell apart a learner who generated the whole artifact from one who used AI to critique their own draft? A rule that treats both identically will either over-punish or go unenforced.\n2. **Disclosure-cost check:** How hard is it for an honest student to comply? If there is no worked example and no fixed format, the cost of disclosing is high and disclosure rates fall, even among willing students.\n3. **Accessibility check:** Does the clause sweep in AI-embedded assistive technology a disabled student depends on? If a screen-reader's AI alt text could violate the policy, the policy is excluding learners, not protecting integrity.\n\nA clause that fails any of these will produce concealment, noncompliance, or exclusion, regardless of how firm it sounds.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A colleague proposes requiring a paragraph-long reflection for every AI use, arguing more reflection means more integrity. What does the disclosure-cost check predict?\" explanation=\"Disclosure rates fall as the cost of disclosing rises. A paragraph-per-use requirement raises that cost far above a one-line format with a worked example, suppressing the very transparency the policy depends on, even among willing students.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Raising disclosure cost suppresses disclosure, undercutting the transparency the policy needs.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Longer reflections always produce more honest students.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It improves accessibility for screen-reader users.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It makes the prohibited act easier for a detector to catch.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misread is that a firmer ban produces more integrity. Firmness and enforceability are different properties; a ban on an undetectable act is loud but empty, and its main effect is to relocate the behavior to where the instructor cannot see it. Strength of wording is not the same as control over outcomes.\n\nA second misread is that disclosure is a punishment students will resist. The opposite holds when the cost of disclosing is low: given a one-line format and a worked example, most students disclose, because the policy reframes AI use as a normal, bounded part of design work rather than a confession. Resistance comes from ambiguity and high disclosure cost, not from disclosure itself.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRewrite a real clause end to end. A department's policy reads: \"AI-generated content is not permitted in any assignment.\" Diagnose it first. It fails distinguishability (alt text and full ghostwriting are both \"AI-generated content\"), it offers no disclosure path, and it has no accessibility carve-out.\n\nNow rebuild around the construct. State the bounded permission: \"You may use AI to brainstorm options, critique your own draft, and generate alternatives to react to. You may not submit AI-generated design work as your own.\" Then lower the disclosure cost with a fixed line and an example: \"For any AI-assisted decision, add one line naming the tool, your prompt intent, and what you accepted or rejected. Example: Used Claude to generate three signaling alternatives for Frame 2; accepted one, rejected two for adding redundant on-screen text.\" Finally, add the accessibility sentence: \"Assistive technologies you rely on for access are always permitted and need no disclosure.\"\n\nThe rebuilt clause is enforceable where the old one was not, because it asks students to make a boundary visible rather than to avoid an undetectable act. The walkthrough is complete when the policy could survive both a disability-services review and a real student trying to comply in good faith.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this section evaluates a policy by its predicted behavioral effect, not its tone. Read a clause and ask what an honest, anxious, or assistive-technology-dependent student would actually do upon reading it. The policy's real meaning is the behavior it produces, not the firmness it projects.\n\nRead any AI-use policy against these four questions:\n\n1. Does it operate on a deterrence model (stop an undetectable act) or a transparency model (keep judgment visible)?\n2. Can it distinguish bounded support from wholesale generation, or does it collapse them into one prohibition?\n3. What is the cost to an honest student of complying, and does a worked example lower it?\n4. Does it protect or penalize a student whose assistive technology embeds AI?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong policy artifact shows the before clause, the specific failure it produced (concealment, exclusion, or unenforceability), and the rewritten clause with its bounded permission, disclosure example, and accessibility carve-out. A reviewer should be able to see that the change converts an undetectable prohibition into a visible boundary, and should be able to predict higher disclosure rates as a result.\n\nA strong rationale sounds like this: \"I replaced 'AI is prohibited' with bounded permission plus a one-line disclosure and example, because the ban produced concealment and excluded an assistive-technology user. I rejected the suggestion to require a paragraph-long reflection for each AI use, because that raises disclosure cost enough to suppress the very transparency the policy depends on.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the policy from a graduate design seminar to a first-year required course with a strict program-wide privacy rule that forbids students from pasting their own data into third-party tools. The transparency goal is unchanged, but a new constraint dominates: even bounded, disclosed use can violate data privacy if the prompt contains protected information. The faithful transfer is to keep bounded permission and disclosure but add a data-handling boundary (\"disclose the tool, but never paste classmate data, identifiable records, or unpublished institutional content\"), and to provide a sanctioned, privacy-reviewed tool option. Copying the seminar's permissive clause without the data rule would satisfy integrity while breaching privacy, trading one compliance failure for another.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/08-04-ai-policy-language-for-students-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"08.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision for AIL 606 08.04. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_ai_policy_language_for_students\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\", \"ail606_supplement_ai_policy_language_for_students\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_policy_language_for_students","stem":"A syllabus clause reads 'Use of AI tools is prohibited. Violations are academic dishonesty.' Which revision best protects student design judgment?","options":["Keep the ban but add 'no exceptions.'","Permit bounded use (brainstorm, self-critique, alternatives), forbid submitting generated work as one's own, and require a one-line disclosure.","Allow any AI use with no disclosure.","Ban AI only during the final week of the course."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Bounded permission with disclosure keeps the learner's judgment visible instead of driving concealment.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ban_ensures_integrity","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_policy_language_for_students"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which disclosure line follows the policy's required format most completely?","options":["I used AI a little.","Used Claude to generate three signaling alternatives for Frame 2; accepted one, rejected two because they added redundant on-screen text.","AI helped with the project.","I confirm I did not cheat."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A complete disclosure names the tool, the prompt intent, and what was accepted or rejected.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_disclosure_without_format","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A student uses a screen reader's AI alt-text generator to access course images. Under a well-written policy, this should be:","options":["A violation, because any AI use is banned.","Permitted assistive use, because the policy distinguishes accessibility tools from generating design work.","Permitted only if the student hides it.","Decided case by case with no written rule."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A policy that names what it protects (the learner's design judgment) does not sweep assistive AI into the prohibition.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_blanket_terms_ignore_access","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ai_policy_language_for_students_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The instructor wants evidence the revised policy worked. Which result is the strongest signal?","options":["Fewer students mentioned AI at all.","Disclosures appeared on 11 of 12 AI-assisted drafts, so the boundary of student judgment was visible and assessable.","The syllabus paragraph got longer.","No student asked any questions about the policy."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Visible disclosures show the policy produced transparency rather than concealment.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_disclosure_without_format","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_ban_ensures_integrity","pattern":"prohibition_guarantees_integrity","description":"Believing a flat ban on AI tools guarantees academic integrity in design work","trigger":"policy states only that AI is prohibited and treats any use as dishonesty","feedback":"A ban drives concealment and cannot distinguish generated work from bounded support; permit bounded use with disclosure.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_disclosure_without_format","pattern":"disclosure_required_without_example","description":"Requiring AI disclosure without giving a concrete format students can follow","trigger":"policy asks for disclosure but provides no example line or fields","feedback":"Show one worked disclosure line (tool, prompt intent, accepted or rejected) so disclosure does not add invention load.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_4_blanket_terms_ignore_access","pattern":"policy_ignores_assistive_ai","description":"Writing a blanket AI clause that does not distinguish assistive tools from generative design work","trigger":"policy sweeps screen-reader or alt-text AI into the same prohibition as generating an artifact","feedback":"Name what the policy protects (the learner's design judgment) so assistive uses are not accidentally banned.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d8cf0623d918d4d7a40279474b3215a0e695890e46810e1b9707a6dde15b1245","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}